<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.6.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00938</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cc60584e-8ad5-409d-a141-8506c18cb60a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <para>This graph represents Andre’s distance from his bicycle as he walks in a park.</para>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of Andre’s distance from his bicycle, coordinate plane, origin \(O\). "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2c0bfc03af451e800041ae81edee70d7127bd687"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Decide whether the following statements are true or false.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph has multiple horizontal intercepts.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> True</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>A horizontal intercept of the graph represents the time when Andre was with his bike.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> True</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>A maximum of the graph is the point \((17,1)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> False</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph represents the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, as a function of time. </item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Function on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ad82b275cc44da777b992c3b1ffd859d0fb1ce42"/></media></figure></para>
<para>At what hour in the afternoon does the maximum of the graph occur? Do not enter commas or spaces in your answer.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>3 p.m.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Match each feature of the situation with a corresponding statement in function notation.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Function on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3942afdc2f914a9eeb7201344ee56cc0a9ee3e7f"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(h(0)= 7\)</item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Maximum height</item>
  <item>Minimum height</item>
  <item>Height staying the same</item>
  <item>Starting height</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis> Starting height</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(h(1.5)\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Maximum height</item>
  <item>Minimum height</item>
  <item>Height staying the same</item>
  <item>Starting height</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Minimum height</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(h(4)\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Maximum height</item>
  <item>Minimum height</item>
  <item>Height staying the same</item>
  <item>Starting height</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis> Maximum height </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(h(t)=6\) for \(7\leq\;\mathrm t\;\leq8\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Maximum height</item>
  <item>Minimum height</item>
  <item>Height staying the same</item>
  <item>Starting height</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis> Height staying the same</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph of \(f(x) =x^2\) is provided.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF THE PARENT FUNCTION Y EQUALS X SQUARED, WITH THE POINTS (1, 1), (2, 4), AND (3, 9) LABELED."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8e96865debed8ceca9104cc834a822c518a3d995"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is \(f(2)\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The value of \(f(x)\) when \(x\) is 2, or \(f(2) = 4\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(f(1)\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(1) = 1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Shannon is running on a flat, rocky trail that eventually rises up a steep mountain. The graph below represents the total distance she covers with respect to time.<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6ac1da9769751f767c0c0f55336f1a0ddce8b602"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>How far does Shannon run in 60 minutes?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3 miles</para></section>
</content>
